    <body>
      <sec id="article-29791.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Swyer-James-MacLeod syndrome is a rare, underrecognized pulmonary disorder characterized by unilateral hyperlucency resulting from reduced pulmonary vascularity and alveolar hyperdistention, most commonly affecting the left lung and variably associated with bronchiectasis. The condition represents a postinfectious sequela of childhood bronchiolitis obliterans, typically following severe lower respiratory tract infections, such as from adenovirus. Impaired bronchiolar development leads to airflow limitation, air trapping, and hypoplasia of the ipsilateral pulmonary arterial bed. Identified risk factors include severe early-life respiratory infection and recurrent childhood pneumonia. Clinical manifestations range from incidental radiographic detection in asymptomatic individuals to recurrent respiratory infections, exertional dyspnea, chronic cough, or hemoptysis.</p>
        <p>Diagnosis is suggested by chest radiography demonstrating unilateral hyperlucency and confirmed with computed tomography showing air trapping, diminished vascular markings, and associated bronchiectasis. Management is primarily supportive and includes vaccination, bronchodilator therapy when indicated, prompt treatment of infections, and surgical resection in selected cases. Potential complications include progressive bronchiectasis, recurrent infections, and, rarely, pulmonary hypertension. Prognosis is generally favorable in mild cases but depends on the extent of structural lung damage.</p>
        <p>This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to sharpen learners' skills in evaluating and managing&#x000a0;Swyer-James-MacLeod syndrome. Participants will deepen their understanding of the condition's etiology, risk factors, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, and&#x000a0;best diagnostic and therapeutic practices.&#x000a0;Greater competence will empower clinicians to collaborate with interprofessional teams&#x000a0;caring for affected individuals.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Assess patients for Swyer-James-MacLeod syndrome based on clinical history, characteristic radiographic and computed tomographic findings, pulmonary function testing, and exclusion of alternative causes of unilateral hyperlucent lung.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply best practices for managing&#x000a0;Swayer-James-MacLeod syndrome and mitigating its potential complications.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Improve understanding of Swyer-James-MacLeod syndrome, its clinical course, and strategies for patients and families to prevent complications and optimize quality of life.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with the interprofessional team to treat and monitor individuals with Swyer-James-MacLeod syndrome, as well as&#x000a0;educate patients and their families,&#x000a0;thereby improving overall health outcomes.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=29791&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=29791">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29791.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Swyer-James-MacLeod syndrome (SJMS), also&#x000a0;known as Swyer-James syndrome or hyperlucent lung syndrome, represents unilateral functional hypoplasia of the pulmonary vasculature and alveolar overdistention, with or without concomitant bronchiectasis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r2">[2]</xref> The condition was independently described in the 1950s by William Mathieson Macleod in England (1954) and by Paul Robert Swyer, alongside radiologist George James, in Canada (1953).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Early reports also described the disorder under eponyms such as &#x0201c;Bret syndrome&#x0201d; and, more rarely, &#x0201c;Janus syndrome,&#x0201d; though Swyer, James, and MacLeod&#x02019;s publications became the widely adopted reference.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>Radiographically, the disorder is defined by hyperlucency of a single pulmonary lobe or the entire lung. Pathogenesis is attributed to postinfectious sequelae of childhood bronchiolitis obliterans, resulting in impaired bronchiolar development, regional air trapping, and diminished vascular perfusion.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29791.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>SJMS was initially considered congenital, attributed to inborn hypoplasia of the pulmonary artery. Although the definitive etiology remains uncertain, the condition is now recognized as a rare sequela of childhood respiratory infections, most frequently bronchiolitis obliterans or pneumonitis. Viral pathogens implicated include measles, respiratory syncytial virus, influenza A, <italic toggle="yes">Paramyxovirus morbillivirus</italic>, and adenovirus types 3, 7, and 21. Bacterial agents associated with recurrent pulmonary infections include<italic toggle="yes"> Bordetella pertussis, Mycobacterium tuberculosis,</italic> and <italic toggle="yes">Mycoplasma pneumoniae</italic>.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r8">[8]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29791.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Due to its rarity, the overall incidence of SJMS remains poorly defined. Although first characterized in 1953, the literature consists primarily of individual case reports and small case series.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r10">[10]</xref> A prevalence of 0.01% was reported among 17,450 reviewed chest radiographs.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r11">[11]</xref> Diagnostic challenges contribute to underdiagnosis, with many cases misclassified as asthma or bronchiectasis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r12">[12]</xref> SJMS has been observed in approximately 4% of patients with bronchiolitis obliterans. The left lung is preferentially affected in most cases for reasons that remain unclear.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r13">[13]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29791.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>SJMS is considered a consequence of postinfectious obliterative bronchiolitis. Bronchiolitis obliterans, also termed "obliterative bronchiolitis," constitutes a clinical syndrome of irreversible small airway obstruction unresponsive to bronchodilators. The condition results from injury to the bronchiolar epithelium and, in some cases, adjacent alveoli, occurring primarily during early childhood and disrupting normal alveolar duct development.</p>
        <p>Respiratory infections represent the principal etiologic factor. Although complete recovery is possible, the repair process may induce excessive granulation tissue proliferation, causing narrowing or complete obliteration of the small airways. Recurrent unilateral obstruction involving terminal bronchi and bronchioles early in life triggers an inflammatory response, producing submucosal fibrosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;The resulting luminal irregularities exacerbate airway occlusion, rendering obstruction more permanent.</p>
        <p>Airway obliteration leads to alveolar air trapping and progressive emphysematous changes, characterized by permanent overdistention of airspaces distal to terminal bronchioles. Destruction of alveolar walls diminishes elastic recoil, promoting airway collapse during exhalation and further air trapping. Consequently, unilateral hyperlucency is typically observed on radiographs.</p>
        <p>A major contributor to the radiographic hyperlucency of a single hemithorax is reduced pulmonary blood flow. Fibrosis of the interalveolar septa obstructs the pulmonary capillary bed, diminishing perfusion to the major pulmonary artery branches. Alveolar hyperinflation, as observed in emphysema, increases mechanical resistance to capillary blood flow. Arterial development is disrupted during early childhood, resulting in hypoplastic pulmonary vasculature.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r15">[15]</xref></p>
        <p>Reduced ventilation of the affected lung or segment induces compensatory vasoconstriction of pulmonary vessels, functionally restricting arterial flow and further contributing to radiographic hyperlucency.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r16">[16]</xref> Functional hypoplasia of the bronchial vasculature may impair lung growth, producing a lung that is either small or normal in size. In some patients with SJMS, bronchiectasis develops proximal to areas affected by bronchiolitis obliterans, likely as a sequela of chronic infections.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29791.s6" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>Histopathological studies in SJMS are limited and derive exclusively from cases requiring surgical intervention. Histology of affected lung tissue frequently demonstrates emphysema with dilated bronchioles, cystic alveolar dilatation, and constrictive bronchiolitis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r17">[17]</xref> Emphysematous changes are typically patchy and may be panacinar. Additional findings include mucus plugging and chronic inflammation. Some studies have reported hypoplasia of the pulmonary artery and alveoli.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;However, imaging evidence of pulmonary vascular hypoplasia may be primarily functional, reflecting reduced blood flow, as pathological examination of resected lungs in SJMS has revealed pulmonary arteries of normal or only slightly reduced caliber.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29791.s7" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Many patients with SJMS remain asymptomatic, while others experience recurrent pulmonary infections. Diagnosis occurs in childhood in some cases and is incidental in adulthood during evaluation for unrelated conditions, such as asthma or recurrent chest infections.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r19">[19]</xref> The disorder presents a diagnostic challenge because clinical manifestations are often nonspecific and may mimic other respiratory conditions, including congenital lobar emphysema (CLE), pneumothorax, localized pulmonary interstitial emphysema, bronchopulmonary sequestration, and Poland syndrome.</p>
        <p>Symptomatic patients most commonly present with recurrent pulmonary infections or exertional dyspnea.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r21">[21]</xref> Additional manifestations include wheezing, reduced exercise tolerance, productive or nonproductive cough with or without hemoptysis, and pleuritic chest pain. Despite these possibilities, the majority of patients remain asymptomatic.</p>
        <p>Physical examination frequently reveals reduced chest expansion, hyperresonance on percussion, and decreased breath sounds on the affected side. Mediastinal shift toward the involved hemithorax may result in displacement of the apex beat.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r22">[22]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29791.s8" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>SJMS exhibits a broad spectrum of clinical presentations. No standardized guidelines exist for diagnostic work-up due to the condition&#x02019;s rarity.</p>
        <p>Posteroanterior chest radiography is typically the 1st-line investigation. Imaging reveals reduced bronchovascular markings, a small hilar shadow, and hyperlucency of the affected segment or lobe. These features become more pronounced on expiration, as the diaphragm on the involved side rises less due to partial lung deflation, highlighting air trapping. Expiratory imaging accentuates differences in transradiancy, with the normal lung appearing more opaque as it fully deflates. The mediastinum may shift toward the affected hemithorax during inspiration and toward the normal side during expiration.</p>
        <p>Computed tomography (CT) of the chest is the preferred modality for assessing the extent and distribution of SJMS. CT imaging may reveal emphysema, bullae, bronchiectasis, atelectasis, and scarring. Previous studies have demonstrated that CT is more sensitive than plain radiography, frequently detecting bilateral lung involvement and inhomogeneous parenchymal changes. Expiratory imaging should be included in the scanning protocol to demonstrate air trapping clearly.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r23">[23]</xref></p>
        <p>Bronchiectasis occurs in approximately 30% of patients with SJMS and may be cylindrical or saccular. Differentiation between these types is important, as it influences clinical course and prognosis. Saccular bronchiectasis is associated with more severe exacerbations and poorer outcomes.&#x000a0;High-resolution chest CT is recommended to confirm the diagnosis when clinical features suggest bronchiectasis.&#x000a0;This modality also ensures detection of subtle changes and allows accurate differentiation from conventional CT findings. CT pulmonary angiography is not routinely required but can reveal hypoplasia of the pulmonary vasculature in the involved lung or segment.</p>
        <p>A ventilation&#x02013;perfusion (V/Q) scan may demonstrate a unilateral matched V/Q defect in the affected area. Some authors advocate this modality as the 1st-line investigation after chest radiography in individuals with suspected SJMS.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r24">[24]</xref>&#x000a0;Pulmonary function testing in SJMS commonly demonstrates a restrictive pattern. Patients with coexisting bronchiectasis may exhibit a mixed restrictive&#x02013;obstructive or predominantly obstructive defect, with airflow limitation unresponsive to bronchodilators.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r25">[25]</xref> Total lung capacity may be mildly reduced. Screening for conditions such as &#x003b1;1-antitrypsin deficiency can exclude rare inherited causes of bronchiectasis or emphysema.</p>
        <p>The diagnostic criteria for SJMS require at least 1 of the following findings:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Unilateral loss of lung volume with hyperlucency on chest radiography</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Unilateral reduction in pulmonary vascularity on chest CT</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Unilateral perfusion defect on technetium-99m V/Q scintigraphy&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r26">[26]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>SJMS often remains underdiagnosed because symptoms can be subtle or absent, and radiographic findings may mimic other pulmonary diseases. Clinicians must maintain a high index of suspicion and correlate multiple diagnostic modalities.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29791.s9" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Management of SJMS is patient-centered and delivered through an interprofessional team approach. Conservative strategies constitute the mainstay of therapy, starting with the prevention and&#x000a0;prompt treatment of recurrent respiratory infections. Primary prevention includes pneumococcal and influenza vaccination. Mucolytic therapy may benefit patients with coexisting bronchiectasis. Chest physiotherapy, incorporating percussion and postural drainage techniques, facilitates effective sputum clearance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r27">[27]</xref>&#x000a0;Inhaled corticosteroids and bronchodilators&#x000a0;may be used as adjunctive agents to manage airflow limitation.&#x000a0;Long-term oxygen therapy is indicated in cases of respiratory failure. Referral to pulmonary rehabilitation programs can optimize respiratory function and improve overall quality of life.</p>
        <p>Surgical intervention is indicated for recurrent pulmonary infections, failure to respond to conservative management, or inadequate symptom control despite optimal therapy. Options include pneumonectomy or lung volume reduction procedures, such as bullectomy, lobectomy, or segmentectomy. These interventions have been performed without significant morbidity and frequently result in substantial improvement in symptoms and respiratory mechanics. Lung volume reduction surgery is particularly effective in patients with marked hyperinflation secondary to emphysema, as it improves the mechanics of the airways, diaphragm, and chest wall.</p>
        <p>Severe or difficult-to-control hemoptysis is uncommon. When present,&#x000a0;this manifestation&#x000a0;may be managed effectively with bronchial artery embolization.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r28">[28]</xref></p>
        <p>A novel approach has been proposed in a single study, in which the main bronchus is occluded while preserving the lung <italic toggle="yes">in situ</italic>. This procedure is potentially less traumatic than pneumonectomy, as it is performed through a small thoracotomy without entry into the pleural space. The disconnected lung acts as a biological prosthesis, preventing herniation, hyperexpansion of the remaining healthy lung, and mediastinal displacement. Infection risk is mitigated through bronchial washings and administration of high-dose, broad-spectrum antibiotics before and after surgery. Fibroatelectasis of the occluded lung is expected within 2 years, with resolution of inflammation. This strategy may be particularly applicable in SJMS, as absent blood flow to the affected lung prevents hypoxemia or right-to-left shunting that would occur if a healthy lung were occluded.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r29">[29]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29791.s10" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>Unilateral hyperlucency of the lung may result from reduced pulmonary blood flow or hyperinflation of the terminal airways. Conditions exhibiting 1 or both features can mimic SJMS, the differential diagnosis of which encompasses both congenital and acquired lung disorders.</p>
        <p>CLE is a developmental disorder of the lower respiratory tract characterized by alveolar overdistention within 1 or more pulmonary lobes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r30">[30]</xref>&#x000a0;The condition arises from abnormalities in bronchopulmonary development. Approximately 25% of cases result from obstruction of the developing airway early in life, producing air trapping and progressive terminal airway hyperinflation via a &#x0201c;ball-valve&#x0201d; mechanism, in which more air enters the affected lung segments during inspiration than exits during expiration. Chest radiographs typically reveal distension of the affected lobe, mediastinal shift with compression and atelectasis of the contralateral lung, and flattening of the diaphragm due to hyperinflation.</p>
        <p>Localized pulmonary interstitial emphysema resembles CLE but develops as a complication of mechanical ventilation at high airway pressures in infants.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r31">[31]</xref>&#x000a0;The disorder may also result from obstruction of larger airways by a foreign body or mass, such as a bronchogenic cyst, bronchopulmonary sequestration, congenital pulmonary airway malformation, congenital diaphragmatic hernia, or pulmonary artery sling. Airway collapse can occur in the presence of a structurally floppy airway, as seen in congenital bronchial atresia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r32">[32]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r33">[33]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r34">[34]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r35">[35]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r36">[36]</xref></p>
        <p>Pulmonary developmental abnormalities include pulmonary agenesis, aplasia, and hypoplasia, in which a lobe or lung, its bronchi, or its vasculature fails to develop completely or partially.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r37">[37]</xref> These defects can produce unilateral hyperexpansion and a radiographic appearance of hyperlucency closely resembling that of SJMS.</p>
        <p>Poland syndrome comprises a spectrum of congenital chest wall anomalies of uncertain etiology. Features include hypoplasia or agenesis of anterior chest wall muscles (pectoralis major, pectoralis minor, latissimus dorsi, serratus anterior, and intercostal muscles), hypoplasia or absence of ipsilateral ribs, and hypoplasia or aplasia of the ipsilateral mammary tissue.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r38">[38]</xref> Radiographically, this syndrome presents as a unilateral hyperlucent hemithorax, which may mimic SJMS.</p>
        <p>SJMS can produce sudden dyspnea, chest discomfort, or hypoxemia, mimicking the presentation of an acute pulmonary embolism. These overlapping clinical features necessitate consideration of pulmonary embolism in the differential diagnosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r39">[39]</xref></p>
        <p>In severe cases, SJMS may be mistaken for pneumothorax, with reduced breath sounds and hyperlucency on chest radiographs. Careful evaluation of imaging, particularly chest CT, combined with clinical assessment, allows accurate diagnosis and prevents unnecessary chest tube placement.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29791.s11" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Patients with SJMS generally have a normal life expectancy. The clinical course varies according to the presence and type of bronchiectasis. Mild or absent cylindrical bronchiectasis is associated with minimal respiratory symptoms and often improves spontaneously. Saccular bronchiectasis predisposes to recurrent pneumonia and represents the highest risk group for requiring surgical intervention.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29791.s12" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>The major complication of SJMS is the development of bronchiectasis, often accompanied by chronic productive cough. Bronchiectasis increases susceptibility to recurrent respiratory infections. The presence of bullae further elevates the risk of spontaneous pneumothorax and may restrict activities such as diving and air travel.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r40">[40]</xref> Rare complications include lung abscess and intractable hemoptysis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r41">[41]</xref></p>
        <p>Progressive decline in lung function may lead to type I respiratory failure, necessitating long-term oxygen therapy. This deterioration significantly impacts patient independence and overall quality of life.</p>
        <p>Emerging evidence suggests a potential association between SJMS and pulmonary hypertension.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r42">[42]</xref>&#x000a0;The presence of pulmonary arterial hypoplasia, combined with reduced functional parenchyma and poorly ventilated areas, leads to hypoxic vasoconstriction and contributes to sustained increases in pulmonary vascular resistance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r43">[43]</xref> Elevated pressures in the pulmonary circulation impose strain on the right ventricle, potentially resulting in pulmonary hypertension.</p>
        <p>Surgical interventions carry risks, including infection, nerve injury, pneumothorax, emphysema, and surgical emphysema. These risks are minimal and are further reduced by minimally invasive techniques such as video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r44">[44]</xref>&#x000a0;Reported cases demonstrate no postoperative morbidity, with&#x000a0;significant improvement in respiratory function following surgery.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29791.s13" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Patient education for SJMS should include a clear explanation of the condition&#x02019;s etiology. The syndrome results from childhood infections rather than congenital or genetic factors and is not heritable, having no implications for family planning. Reassurance should be provided that the disorder is not the result of patient actions and could not have been prevented. Smoking cessation is strongly advised to reduce further decline in lung function, limit progression of emphysema, and prevent the development of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease.</p>
        <p>Education should likewise emphasize early recognition of respiratory infections, prompt initiation of treatment, and maintenance of a rescue antibiotic pack at home. Influenza and pneumococcal vaccination are essential preventive measures. Inhaler technique and adherence should be assessed during follow-up, and patients should receive contact information for their community nurse. Referral for chest physiotherapy is recommended, accompanied by instruction on the importance of regular chest-clearing exercises to optimize respiratory function.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29791.s14" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>SJMS is not considered congenital or genetically transmitted and, therefore, should not influence family planning. However, data regarding pregnancy outcomes in women with this condition remain limited. The incidence of SJMS in pregnancy has been reported as 1:2,500.&#x000a0;(Source:&#x000a0;<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://search.catalog.loc.gov/instances/b84ef66c-6c25-59ea-a71e-d78859a7ed2d?option=lccn&#x00026;query=65012650">Mount Sinai Hospital, New York</ext-link>, 1965) Recent case reports describe favorable outcomes with uncomplicated deliveries and no significant maternal respiratory complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r45">[45]</xref></p>
        <p>However, lung abnormalities associated with SJMS may theoretically contribute to maternal hypoxia, increasing the risk of placental insufficiency, preeclampsia, fetal growth restriction, and intrauterine fetal demise.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r46">[46]</xref> Maternal oxygenation should be monitored throughout pregnancy, and a 3rd-trimester growth scan is recommended for fetal surveillance.</p>
        <p>Early epidural analgesia may be prudent during delivery. Evidence suggests that the use of this intervention&#x000a0;in women with respiratory disease reduces minute ventilation and oxygen consumption, thereby mitigating maternal respiratory stress.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29791.r47">[47]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29791.s15" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Clinicians in outpatient, inpatient, and emergency settings should maintain SJMS in the differential diagnosis for adult or pediatric patients presenting with shortness of breath. A hyperlucent lung field on radiography warrants careful evaluation, as early and accurate diagnosis prevents unnecessary invasive procedures, such as chest drain insertion for misdiagnosed pneumothorax, and reduces progression of lung dysfunction.</p>
        <p>Recurrent pulmonary infections represent the most common and concerning long-term complication of SJMS. Management includes sputum culture, prompt and effective antibiotic therapy, and symptomatic care. In most patients, airway clearance is maintained through chest physiotherapy and administration of inhaled bronchodilators and&#x000a0;low-dose inhaled corticosteroids. Lung volume reduction surgery, including techniques such as pneumonectomy, lobectomy, or segmentectomy, has been shown in recent studies to improve pulmonary function and alleviate symptoms.</p>
        <p>Optimal care requires an interprofessional approach. Early referral for chest physiotherapy and timely cardiothoracic consultation can prevent serious complications, including lung abscess and sepsis, while improving quality of life. Pharmacists provide prescription review, inhaler education, and drug-drug interaction checks. Nurses monitor patient status and report changes to the managing clinicians.</p>
      </sec>
